<Base
  title="About — Relief Globe"
  description="How Relief Globe is made: open elevation data, ray-traced relief, and the data licenses behind it."
>
  <article class="about">
    <a class="back" href="/">← All countries</a>
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      <strong>Relief Globe</strong> is a ray-traced relief map of every country, rendered
      from open elevation data and served as a static site. The look follows the
      “3D-render topographic” aesthetic — soft ray-traced shadows, heavy vertical
      exaggeration, warm land tones over a desaturated teal sea with bathymetry.
    </p>

    <h2>How it’s made</h2>
    <p>Each hero is a physically-based render, not a hillshade filter:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Fusion.</strong> Copernicus GLO-30 land elevation is fused with GEBCO
        bathymetry into one seamless heightfield — the sea floor is part of the picture,
        so shelf seas and ocean trenches read as terrain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Render.</strong> The heightfield drives displacement in Blender’s Cycles
        renderer (OptiX, with OpenImageDenoise), lit by a low sun for long relief shadows,
        coloured by elevation- and depth-keyed ramps.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Borders.</strong> Natural Earth boundaries are composited on top as crisp
        vector lines — never baked into the terrain — so they stay sharp and can be toggled.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Snow.</strong> A permanent snow/ice mask (ESA WorldCover, class 70) whitens
        glaciers and ice caps.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      Coming next: an interactive globe draping pre-shaded raster tiles, served via MapLibre
      GL and PMTiles.
    </p>

    <h2>Boundaries &amp; worldview</h2>
    <p>
      Borders and coastlines use Natural Earth’s <strong>de-facto</strong> worldview, applied
      consistently everywhere. Disputed boundaries and lines of control are drawn
      <strong>dashed</strong>, as are maritime-indicator lines. This is a cartographic default,
      not a political statement.
    </p>

    <h2>Data</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Role</th><th>License</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Copernicus DEM GLO-30</td><td>Land elevation</td><td>Copernicus free/open license</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GEBCO 2026 Grid</td><td>Bathymetry</td><td>Public domain</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ESA WorldCover 2021 v200</td><td>Permanent snow/ice mask</td><td>CC-BY 4.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Natural Earth</td><td>Borders &amp; coastlines</td><td>Public domain</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="attrib">
      <li>Contains modified Copernicus DEM data (source WorldDEM-30, © DLR e.V. and © Airbus
        Defence and Space; provided under COPERNICUS by the European Union and ESA).</li>
      <li>Reproduced from the GEBCO_2026 Grid, GEBCO Compilation Group (2026).</li>
      <li>© ESA WorldCover project 2021 / Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data (2021)
        processed by the ESA WorldCover consortium.</li>
      <li>Natural Earth — <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com">naturalearthdata.com</a> (public domain).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tools &amp; technique</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rendering</strong> — <a href="https://www.blender.org">Blender</a> (Cycles, OptiX, OpenImageDenoise).</li>
      <li><strong>Geoprocessing</strong> — GDAL, rasterio, WhiteboxTools.</li>
      <li><strong>Web</strong> — <a href="https://maplibre.org">MapLibre GL JS</a> (globe projection), <a href="https://github.com/protomaps/PMTiles">PMTiles</a> (Protomaps).</li>
      <li><strong>Technique</strong> — shaded relief in Blender after Daniel Huffman’s canonical
        method; aesthetic reference: Frank Ramspott, “3D Render Topographic Map — Neutral.”</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Made with AI</h2>
    <p>
      Built in collaboration with AI: the pipeline code, documentation, and Blender rendering
      setup were developed in a pair-programming workflow with Anthropic’s Claude (via Claude
      Code). Data-source selection, design decisions, and final review are the author’s.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

